So the HTML now contains a new top‑level card (`.featured-card`) that is separate from the `.people-grid` grid of other cards. The JavaScript that I injected earlier (the reveal script) was written to target elements like:

```js
document.querySelectorAll('.section-label, .section h2, .section-sub, .mission blockquote, .accent-line, .mission > p, .mission-goal, .footer p')
    .forEach(el => el.classList.add('reveal'));

document.querySelectorAll('.people-grid').forEach(function (grid) {
    Array.prototype.forEach.call(grid.children, function (card, i) {
        card.classList.add('reveal');
        card.style.setProperty('--d', (Math.min(i, 24) * 0.04) + 's');
    });
});
```

That code still assumes that every card that should be revealed lives inside a `.people-grid`. However, after the overhaul the Glint Research card is now outside that grid, in its own `.featured-card` container. Consequently, the script will never add the `reveal` class (or the staggered delay variable) to the featured card, meaning it won’t animate on scroll. Also, the selector list for the other sections remains fine, but the part that specifically targets the grid children needs to be expanded to include the new `.featured-card` element.

I could immediately edit the HTML to wrap the featured card back into the grid, but that would defeat the purpose of the redesign – the featured card is meant to be a distinct, highlighted element. The proper fix is to update the JavaScript so that it also selects `.featured-card` (and perhaps any future special cards) and applies the same reveal logic.

